From: Patrik Eriksson <patrik.m.eriksson@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 16:27:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] web: APERTAR_WEB_PREVIEW_RECORDING_FPS caps or pauses the
 web preview while a raw take runs

Why: when a browser shows the live preview, captureWebPreviewFrame downsamples the 1280x720
preview to 640x360 on libcamera's completion thread, inside the serial per-frame cycle, for
every frame at up to 30 fps. The kb's research (working/dng-chain, R5, R7, S2) named it a
candidate for the frames lost at capture in short takes with the WebUI open; verification
(V/H4) found the kb had already profiled it at about 0.2 ms per frame idle (0.3 to 0.5 ms while
recording), too small to explain those gaps alone. It stays the one per-frame image pass on that
thread, so a cap or pause while recording is a cheap, measurable mitigation.

What: a lock-free CameraController::rawTakeActive() (the write queue's gauge flag) tells the
completion thread that a take runs; the web preview is then rate-limited to
APERTAR_WEB_PREVIEW_RECORDING_FPS (default 30, unchanged; 0 = no web preview frames while
recording; values below 0.5 count as 0; non-finite values fall back to 30). README row.

Tested: both files syntax-check (CameraController.cpp with the libcamera block on, no new
errors). Reviewed (V/PATCHES.md): the NaN case is now rejected and the accessor sits after
frameBufferStatus. Not run on the Pi: the two-take test (60 s with the WebUI closed, then
open) decides whether the knob matters.

